Example 1 – same denominator
  Calculate &frac{3}{8} + &frac{2}{8}
  Same denominator: add numerators
  3 + 2 = 5
  &frac{5}{8}
  = &frac{5}{8}
  (When denominators match, just add the tops.)

Example 2 – different denominators
  Calculate &frac{2}{3} + &frac{1}{4}
  LCM of 3 and 4 is 12
  &frac{2}{3} = &frac{8}{12}; &frac{1}{4} = &frac{3}{12}
  &frac{8}{12} + &frac{3}{12} = &frac{11}{12}
  = &frac{11}{12}
  (Convert both to twelfths, then add.)

Add or subtract. Simplify if possible.

(a) &frac{2}{7} + &frac{3}{7}
(b) &frac{5}{8} − &frac{1}{8}
(c) &frac{4}{9} + &frac{2}{9}
(d) &frac{7}{10} − &frac{3}{10}

Find a common denominator, then subtract.

(a) &frac{3}{4} − &frac{1}{3}
(b) &frac{5}{6} − &frac{2}{3}
(c) &frac{7}{8} − &frac{1}{2}
(d) &frac{4}{5} − &frac{1}{4}

Convert to improper fractions, calculate, convert back.

(a) 1&frac{1}{2} + 2&frac{1}{4}
(b) 3&frac{1}{3} − 1&frac{1}{2}
(c) 2&frac{2}{5} + 1&frac{3}{10}
(d) 4&frac{1}{6} − 2&frac{2}{3}

Calculate. Give answers in simplest form.

(a) &frac{1}{2} + &frac{1}{3} + &frac{1}{6}
(b) &frac{5}{6} − &frac{1}{4} + &frac{1}{3}
(c) 2&frac{3}{4} − 1&frac{1}{3}
(d) &frac{7}{10} + &frac{3}{5} − &frac{1}{2}
(e) 3&frac{1}{2} − &frac{2}{3} − &frac{5}{6}

✓ &frac{1}{3} + &frac{1}{4} = &frac{7}{12}
✗ &frac{1}{3} + &frac{1}{4} = &frac{2}{7}
✓ You need a common denominator to add fractions
✗ &frac{3}{4} − &frac{1}{2} = &frac{2}{2}
✓ Always simplify your final answer

